WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.820
Two searches each return three results.

00:00:03.995 --> 00:00:06.358
One examined every stored vector.

00:00:06.508 --> 00:00:08.111
The other took a shortcut and missed a

00:00:08.131 --> 00:00:09.528
closer candidate.

00:00:09.704 --> 00:00:11.552
Could you tell which search ran just by

00:00:11.572 --> 00:00:13.175
counting the results on screen?

00:00:13.351 --> 00:00:14.348
Make a prediction.

00:00:16.757 --> 00:00:19.346
The output count does not reveal the work

00:00:19.366 --> 00:00:20.879
or the guarantee behind it.

00:00:21.042 --> 00:00:23.277
We need to separate the comparison rule,

00:00:23.297 --> 00:00:25.339
the search procedure, and the number of

00:00:25.359 --> 00:00:26.562
leaders requested.

00:00:27.471 --> 00:00:29.643
An inverted index can jump from an

00:00:29.663 --> 00:00:32.272
analysed term to documents containing it.

00:00:32.421 --> 00:00:34.837
A dense vector has no equivalent posting

00:00:34.857 --> 00:00:37.156
list for all passages expressing a

00:00:37.176 --> 00:00:39.824
meaning. Its dimensions do not directly

00:00:39.844 --> 00:00:42.581
correspond to recognisable words, and a

00:00:42.601 --> 00:00:45.777
useful passage need not share query terms.

00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:49.527
The exact baseline is flat vector search:

00:00:49.547 --> 00:00:51.332
compare the query with every stored

00:00:51.352 --> 00:00:53.870
vector. Its work grows with collection

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:55.957
size and dimensionality.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.509
It can construct a complete ranking or

00:00:58.529 --> 00:01:00.545
retain only the leaders.

00:01:01.447 --> 00:01:03.895
Use the chapter's six points and put the

00:01:03.915 --> 00:01:05.608
query at zero, zero.

00:01:05.783 --> 00:01:08.714
This example uses Euclidean distance, not

00:01:08.734 --> 00:01:10.853
the cosine rule from the first film.

00:01:11.015 --> 00:01:12.734
Smaller distance wins.

00:01:12.897 --> 00:01:15.544
Exhaustive search examines A through F and

00:01:15.564 --> 00:01:18.508
returns A, B and C.

00:01:19.075 --> 00:01:22.870
A is one unit away, B two, and C about two

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:23.952
point eight three.

00:01:24.127 --> 00:01:26.633
D is about three point one six away.

00:01:26.795 --> 00:01:29.507
Exact here means exact under the chosen

00:01:29.527 --> 00:01:30.692
comparison rule.

00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:32.857
It does not guarantee that those nearest

00:01:32.877 --> 00:01:35.602
points represent relevant passages.

00:01:36.508 --> 00:01:39.579
Now stipulate a run that examines only A,

00:01:39.625 --> 00:01:44.759
B, D and F. It returns A, B and D.

00:01:45.076 --> 00:01:47.350
C was closer than D, but was never

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.876
examined. Both runs still requested the

00:01:49.896 --> 00:01:51.267
same top three.

00:01:51.838 --> 00:01:53.646
Approximate nearest-neighbour structures

00:01:53.666 --> 00:01:56.687
reduce search work by exploring selected

00:01:56.707 --> 00:01:58.555
regions or neighbours.

00:01:58.731 --> 00:02:01.068
They may miss a true nearest neighbour.

00:02:01.243 --> 00:02:03.156
This small example illustrates one

00:02:03.176 --> 00:02:06.997
possible miss, not a measured system, an H

00:02:07.017 --> 00:02:09.806
N S W simulation, or a claim that

00:02:09.826 --> 00:02:12.151
approximate search always misses.

00:02:13.047 --> 00:02:16.399
H N S W is one widely used approximate

00:02:16.419 --> 00:02:19.092
approach. It organises vectors as a

00:02:19.112 --> 00:02:21.939
navigable graph with multiple layers.

00:02:22.115 --> 00:02:24.015
Connections help a search explore

00:02:24.035 --> 00:02:26.631
promising neighbours instead of comparing

00:02:26.651 --> 00:02:27.803
every vector.

00:02:28.347 --> 00:02:30.146
Our graph is schematic.

00:02:30.321 --> 00:02:31.963
It does not show the algorithm's real

00:02:31.983 --> 00:02:33.831
links or a measured path.

00:02:33.981 --> 00:02:36.718
Other index designs partition the space,

00:02:36.751 --> 00:02:40.030
compress vectors, or combine strategies.

00:02:40.192 --> 00:02:42.775
Indexing changes how the search runs; the

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:45.185
requested k still describes its output

00:02:45.205 --> 00:02:46.099
size.

00:02:46.989 --> 00:02:50.286
Keep four controls apart: who may compete,

00:02:50.552 --> 00:02:52.684
what rule scores them, how the engine

00:02:52.704 --> 00:02:55.724
searches, and how many leaders it retains.

00:02:55.874 --> 00:02:59.191
Our exact and approximate runs requested k

00:02:59.276 --> 00:03:02.413
equals three while examining six and four

00:03:02.433 --> 00:03:03.675
points respectively.

00:03:04.237 --> 00:03:06.986
Top k does not mean only k candidates were

00:03:07.006 --> 00:03:10.362
examined. Exact lexical pruning can safely

00:03:10.382 --> 00:03:12.463
skip candidates that cannot enter the

00:03:12.483 --> 00:03:15.362
leaders; approximate vector search offers

00:03:15.382 --> 00:03:16.689
a different guarantee.

00:03:16.851 --> 00:03:20.220
Lexical, dense and hybrid systems can all

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:21.946
return top-k results.

00:03:22.849 --> 00:03:24.731
Dense retrieval has no comparable

00:03:24.751 --> 00:03:25.968
term-match floor.

00:03:26.117 --> 00:03:28.532
Unless filters narrow the collection,

00:03:28.759 --> 00:03:30.917
every stored vector can receive a

00:03:30.937 --> 00:03:32.321
similarity score.

00:03:32.457 --> 00:03:35.144
An unrelated passage can still be nearest

00:03:35.164 --> 00:03:37.939
when every alternative is farther away.

00:03:38.504 --> 00:03:41.593
Metadata filters, lexical evidence and

00:03:41.613 --> 00:03:44.570
vectors can coexist in one product.

00:03:44.745 --> 00:03:47.714
The candidate boundary and output size

00:03:47.747 --> 00:03:49.634
remain separate questions.

00:03:49.796 --> 00:03:52.715
To assess a search, inspect its filters,

00:03:52.735 --> 00:03:55.717
scoring rule, exact or approximate

00:03:55.737 --> 00:03:58.939
execution, and result boundary—then judge

00:03:58.959 --> 00:04:01.077
whether the returned material is useful.
